0803.2303	open_problem	Does there exist such an Exceptional Field?	It is known that of all imaginary quadratic field {{formula:8fcefc9a-1755-4e03-8908-b95324765e45}}  with class number {{formula:293c917c-800c-406c-bd21-b69618e063f9}} , we have {{formula:d851f82e-a10a-4ed6-924c-8e9fdf019923}} , except for at most one exceptional field, for which {{formula:ed5bb14b-df92-47d1-a9e9-c7f8231c3d1d}}  may be Larger. Does there exist such an Exceptional Field?	0.9
0805.3456	future_work	The extension of the proposed technique for synchronization of nonlinear systems is the subject of ongoing work.	In this paper the problem of synchronizing a network of identical linear systems described by the state-space model {{formula:a7e02dba-0c59-4950-b1eb-3578a5ae1f0b}}  under general interconnection topologies has been addressed. A dynamic controller ensuring exponential convergence of the solutions to a synchronized solution of the decoupled systems is provided assuming that (i) {{formula:f8589f9c-0c74-4e77-bd76-f907131ea839}}  has no exponentially unstable mode, (ii) {{formula:3ffec993-9346-4607-a733-ad410a92ac39}}  is stabilizable and {{formula:7d289923-8c74-497a-81af-4b66f95f6eb7}}  is detectable, and (iii) the communication graph is uniformly connected. Stronger conditions are shown to be sufficient (and, to some extent, also necessary) to ensure synchronization with the often considered static diffusive output coupling. The extension of the proposed technique for synchronization of nonlinear systems is the subject of ongoing work.	0.9
0806.2938	open_problem	No explicit form of the fine tuning is known theoretically but coexistence of the two scale is seen in the lattice simulations.	For the defects to become dynamical degrees of freedom there should be a fine tuning which allows infrared and ultraviolet scales to coexist. Such fine tuning is well understood in the Abelian case, (see Eq. (REF )). A central point: for the dual-superconductor mechanism of confinement to be relevant, similar fine tuning should be realized in the non-Abelian case as well. This time, however, it is self-tuning between the action and entropy of the 2d surfaces. No explicit form of the fine tuning is known theoretically but coexistence of the two scale is seen in the lattice simulations.	0.9
0806.2938	open_problem	Also, there should be consistency between dynamics in four dimensions, or of the original Yang-Mills fields and dynamics on the defects world-sheet.	Also, there should be consistency between dynamics in four dimensions, or of the original Yang-Mills fields and dynamics on the defects world-sheet. Let us conclude with an example of such a consistency check. For dynamics of a particle (or 1d defect) to be in fact independent on the ultraviolet scale the action associated with the 'monopoles' should be approximately {{formula:91b95a3b-bc67-490e-b6cf-f6899af3b929}} see Eq. (REF ). And this has been checked on the lattice {{cite:413a26e8015522e476bfb548de5c35df3fa878d4}}. On the other hand, the radiative mass (REF ) is associated now with the non-Abelian fields which live not in the bulk but on the world-sheet (see discussion above). Which seems to be also true on the lattice, for references and review see {{cite:52306a6f3f8d2b4ad6d3684e31ae66036b171545}}. The condition (REF ) replaces the quantization condition for the magnetic field in case of the non-Abelian monopoles living on the surfaces.	0.7
0807.0023	future_work	Further research in this area may prove useful for other network types such as those generated from other metadata properties.	This paper has provided a preliminary exploration of metadata generation in terms of metadata property propagation within an associative network of repository resources. Further research in this area may prove useful for other network types such as those generated from other metadata properties. For instance, it may be of interest to study the effect of this algorithm on usage networks {{cite:468a23a878b132724286a69804c9d8374e3227f1}}. Usage metadata, unlike citation and journal metadata, is applicable to every accessible resource. It would be interesting to see what co-usage means for a particular genera of resources by determining which metadata properties these networks are best at propagating.	0.9
0807.0023	future_work	"Future work that studies this method with the inclusion of humans that help to validate and “clean"" the recommended metadata would be telling of how much this method is able to speed up the process of generating accurate and reliable metadata for metadata-poor resources."	"What has been presented in this study is the results of this algorithm without the intervention of any human components (besides the initial creation of metadata through the hep-th dataset creation process). Future work that studies this method with the inclusion of humans that help to validate and “clean"" the recommended metadata would be telling of how much this method is able to speed up the process of generating accurate and reliable metadata for metadata-poor resources. Such an analysis is left to future research."	0.95
0807.1134	limitation	For calculations at 30–150 AU around 1–3 M{{formula:bbc857ae-465e-439d-b410-ab04ee1aba90}}  stars, the spacing of mass bins in an annulus and the spacing of annuli in the disk limit the accuracy of the results.	As long as the statistical assumptions underlying the formalism are met, coagulation calculations provide a reasonable representation of real collision evolution {{cite:5236f64017cd2f1c1db97fb6ec2df7ae8ea463d8}}, {{cite:2c64bf6c69e65cb2071f345755326f7896f1aad9}}, {{cite:dd0e5aa3e6d0aa096049e9843835c0f7712906ba}}, {{cite:a2ed2e2f74c181b1efd0087c9b099d945eb86008}}, {{cite:ef113e1b2a6ed7224b5a09878eac5b9dfb8ee795}}, {{cite:b093907d4a3cb5b9dff5174e7820be0800b942b5}}, {{cite:e05b94b0abb0e87284463403d1e211fddb97a564}}, {{cite:c46d22644756616786816ff8243f85a3ef116ca1}}, {{cite:ce9f8737a1f81a7e452b2d91c706364bba394ee4}}, {{cite:2ee1f5fbb05af202d278638441fba201dd7de042}}, {{cite:33dddbfee7450dac3fbefd374b8812b721c5c482}}. For calculations at 30–150 AU around 1–3 M{{formula:bbc857ae-465e-439d-b410-ab04ee1aba90}}  stars, the spacing of mass bins in an annulus and the spacing of annuli in the disk limit the accuracy of the results. Our standard mass spacing, {{formula:70fc209f-d068-432c-b33c-7b139dface75}} , lengthens the evolution time by 10% to 20% relative to more accurate calculations with {{formula:257ac13a-028c-45b4-bbfb-2acd3023d202}}  1.4 {{cite:2ee1f5fbb05af202d278638441fba201dd7de042}}. The radial resolution, {{formula:50ffe053-2d66-4894-b232-762e76852b57}}  = 0.025, also lengthens the evolution time. Compared to calculations described in {{cite:34b2cc4693695ca9f2a682d190e35e7ab035e5d5}}, improvements in our treatment of interactions among particles in neighboring annuli reduce lags by a factor of two, from {{formula:120b00dc-7c7d-489a-a616-7bc9090105e7}}  20% to {{formula:08a08372-60a3-413c-84e4-1c539fc1d85f}}  10%. Combining the lags for mass spacing and radial resolution, our evolution timescales are {{formula:f02c34c8-781d-4e41-b7a2-cfc29235d6dd}}  20% to 30% longer than the actual evolution times. This lag is comparable to the dispersion in timescales derived from multiple calculations with identical starting conditions. Thus, improvements in resolution are unlikely to alter our results significantly.	0.9
0808.2057	open_problem	Since there is no general classification of weighing matrices, characterizing graphs of Hankel weighing matrices seems to be out of reach.	Since there is no general classification of weighing matrices, characterizing graphs of Hankel weighing matrices seems to be out of reach. This could be however done for graphs of degree 4 and 9, on the basis of present knowledge on {{formula:d4a6afcf-30a2-472b-8737-06b8e9fb234c}}  and {{formula:3ebada2b-1957-4fef-bfde-668b53de2673}}  (see {{cite:d2b81348ac8204edd98e7fd48d8bb1df5c9ba744}}).	0.9
0808.2057	future_work	Can one classify the Hankel and anticirculant weighing matrices which cannot be obtained by this trick?	Sections  and  make extensive use of the following trick. Begin with a circulant weighing matrix, then reverse the columns. If the resulting graph has loops, one can create a loopless graph by the “interlacing” trick. In fact the interlaced graph will again be connected if the original graph was connected but not bipartite. Can one classify the Hankel and anticirculant weighing matrices which cannot be obtained by this trick?	0.85
0811.2508	limitation	Surface gravity determinations in particular require better constraints, since these enable age-dating of individual sources.	However, there are areas where improvements in uncertainties are needed and basic assumptions tested. Surface gravity determinations in particular require better constraints, since these enable age-dating of individual sources. In the short term, improvements in spectral models can help in this endeavor; however, a sufficiently sampled grid of benchmark sources may obviate the need for models entirely.	0.9
0811.2508	future_work	Benchmarks should increasingly arise from mass standards, for which age constraints are more precise; these additionally provide necessary tests of evolutionary models upon which most of the age-dating techniques hinge.	Benchmarks should increasingly arise from mass standards, for which age constraints are more precise; these additionally provide necessary tests of evolutionary models upon which most of the age-dating techniques hinge. Improved angular resolution and sensitivity with {{formula:c355ace2-97f7-441c-892b-5b9952550f3e}}  and the next generation of large ({{formula:ea7f78cb-378b-4c98-acd2-38b0a459a0c8}} m) telescopes will increase resolved binary sample sizes by expanding the volume in which they can be found and monitored. These facilities will also aid searches for substellar cluster members in old open field and globular clusters and, perhaps more importantly, mass standards in these clusters to facilitate more stringent tests of evolutionary models (T. Dupuy, priv. comm.). Finally, a larger, more complete sample of brown dwarfs with precise RV measurements will both improve our statistical constraints on the age of the local brown dwarf population (and subpopulations) while additionally aiding in the search for mass standards.	0.85
0811.3859	future_work	For example, it will be interesting to prove a {{formula:bf11a214-943f-4bd0-8256-01184643d69f}}  upper bound for {{formula:a565f4cf-3afd-445c-80fc-35aac302745d}} .	Our results provide new possibilities to attack the graph isomorphism problem. For example, it will be interesting to prove a {{formula:bf11a214-943f-4bd0-8256-01184643d69f}}  upper bound for {{formula:a565f4cf-3afd-445c-80fc-35aac302745d}} . Note that this will imply that {{formula:66ee8bbb-603c-40c3-8233-24ff83ac40b0}} . Similarly, are there special cases of {{formula:b1bc22ba-5df9-41e0-af2d-0e0e2806e501}}  (other than what is translated from the bounds for {{formula:66119676-2026-470d-ad92-ecc2591db094}} ) which can be solved in polynomial time?	0.9
0811.3859	future_work	It will still be interesting to give a better (than the trivial {{formula:13fc76b0-a8a2-468c-86b2-d1f810e3e58f}} ) upper bound for linear matroids represented over fixed finite fields (even for {{formula:e44a632e-ecf4-4a9b-891a-951b45448747}} ).	The representations of the matroid in the definition of {{formula:0e5247ae-769f-42ec-88f9-6256404ec067}}  is over fields of size at least {{formula:fd9e4674-c05f-4b5d-8f5f-4ad51bcf12f1}}  and at most {{formula:5f94f549-04f2-4d28-969a-d2893ccbca5c}} , where {{formula:d54f65eb-9d58-468b-b3ac-c541902d1fc5}}  is the size of the ground set of the matroid. This is critically needed for the observation of {{formula:ed4610c8-dc2d-439e-a414-b855faf3ac0c}} -hardness. One could ask if the problem is easier over fixed finite fields independent on the input. However, we note that, by our results, it follows that this problem over {{formula:d69eaf44-6579-410a-9109-f46d53a58f46}}  is already hard for {{formula:cc8ebbbd-ccaf-4230-89d7-398a70b7caa3}} . It will still be interesting to give a better (than the trivial {{formula:13fc76b0-a8a2-468c-86b2-d1f810e3e58f}} ) upper bound for linear matroids represented over fixed finite fields (even for {{formula:e44a632e-ecf4-4a9b-891a-951b45448747}} ).	0.85
0902.0196	future_work	It would be interesting to see if a corresponding bispectral theory may be constructed there.	Results similar to those in this paper may be established for non-compact, non-commutative groups {{cite:e5b48b73034f1617a7ab390778fb02c052946714}}. Those results rely on the duality theorem of Tatsuuma. The Tannaka-Krein duality theorem, which is central to this paper, has been extended to compact groupoids {{cite:eaa3e57ce51893fa52bc76a583636df7b859}}. It would be interesting to see if a corresponding bispectral theory may be constructed there.	0.9
0902.2504	limitation	On the other hand, those artificial WDB example presented here are intrinsically limited by their small sizewith the largest WDB considered here involving only 70 set names and have restricted structural featureswhich should involve not only nested chains but also nested tree structures, and, in principle, further comprehensive tests should be done to further characterise the usefulness of those practical strategies towards computing bisimulation suggested here.	The main conclusion from the above experiments is that, although bisimulation (crucial to the hyperset approach to WDB and the {{formula:376f4bdb-b4a1-424f-97a1-a6f1fbfd7b85}} -query language) presents some difficulty in efficient and realistic implementation, this problem appears to be resolvable in principle. Moreover, this assertion is somewhat supported by the empirical testing of artificial WDB examples described in Sections REF –REF . In particular, these artificial WDB were chosen to simulate some specific worst case structural features of WDB similarly to physicists conducting some very specific experiments allowing to understand the most fundamental laws of the nature instead of dealing with something complicated as in the real life. On the other hand, those artificial WDB example presented here are intrinsically limited by their small sizewith the largest WDB considered here involving only 70 set names and have restricted structural featureswhich should involve not only nested chains but also nested tree structures, and, in principle, further comprehensive tests should be done to further characterise the usefulness of those practical strategies towards computing bisimulation suggested here. Also, empirical testing of some particular real-world WDB of sufficiently big size is important, but in this case a lot of further work should be done on optimisation of query execution which was outside of the scope of this work but deserves further investigation. We only considered one essential aspect of efficiency for the current version of the query system related with the idea of local/global bisimulation. However, in principle, the experiments done here suggest that these strategies show potential and merit further investigation.	0.9
