0909.5313	limitation	Hence, the above theorem tells us that just the expansion properties of for any -sized are not sufficient to guarantee -distance from every subspace of dimension .	It is well known that for any family of -regular multigraphs (see e.g. ). As a consequence of this lower bound it follows for any fixed group and any multiset that . Hence, the above theorem tells us that just the expansion properties of for any -sized are not sufficient to guarantee -distance from every subspace of dimension . The proof of the above statement can be found in the full version .	0.8
0910.0670	limitation	The UDF described in this paper is systematically constructed, uniquely determined, and in principle exact. However, in terms of real computations, one can only keep terms up to some order in .	The UDF described in this paper is systematically constructed, uniquely determined, and in principle exact. However, in terms of real computations, one can only keep {{formula:8045cec9-9e9f-4cc4-bba1-664a8eb61a3a}}  terms up to some order in {{formula:f740a33f-2833-48ad-95f9-5c32e52afda2}} . A natural question thus arises. How well will the truncated version work? In general, this question can only be answered with numerical results. However, by providing theoretical arguments and comparisons to other approaches, we wish to convey that this method is likely to produce good results and thus to attract computational efforts towards using the proposed approach.	0.9
0910.0670	limitation	However, even if the Luttinger-Ward/Klein functional is computed to all orders, the error in the value of the grand potential (or the ground state energy) due to restriction on the Green's functions remains unknown.	Instead of allowing the physical, full, one-particle Green's function, Dahlen et al. {{cite:0bdcfd8ba557bc50dab69ea4c296373f2c9fb015}} proposed to find that stationary point of the Luttinger-Ward/Klein functional while restricting the argument to the Hartree-Fock Green's functions or the Green's functions of non-interacting systems. However, even if the Luttinger-Ward/Klein functional is computed to all orders, the error in the value of the grand potential (or the ground state energy) due to restriction on the Green's functions remains unknown.	0.8
0910.1692	future_work	Potential future work focuses on several areas including but not limited to data replication, and the storage and mark up of secondary large data such as quark propagators.	Potential future work focuses on several areas including but not limited to data replication, and the storage and mark up of secondary large data such as quark propagators.	0.9
0910.5055	open_problem	It may still be true, however, that under the additional restriction that the Hamiltonian has a constant gap, a polynomial time algorithm exists, even when the bond dimension is as large as polynomial.	It is natural to ask how much the results in this article can be improved. By Ref. {{cite:31c393f3562373f7cb2fdfbbffbde51df0987987}}, we know that no polynomial algorithm exists for finding optimal approximations of polynomial bond dimension (unless P=NP). However, the difficult instances of Ref. {{cite:31c393f3562373f7cb2fdfbbffbde51df0987987}} have a spectral gap of {{formula:4921fd0f-0d24-4978-acb7-002315317358}} . Hastings has shown that ground state of 1D quantum system with a constant gap can be approximated by a MPS with polynomial bond dimension {{cite:efd85deefa25d1c8b9b0c119d2f50222eda99c61}}. However, this is too large to immediately yield an efficient algorithm from our result. It may still be true, however, that under the additional restriction that the Hamiltonian has a constant gap, a polynomial time algorithm exists, even when the bond dimension is as large as polynomial.	0.9
0910.5055	future_work	As mentioned earlier, we leave for further research the question of how this algorithm can be used in combination with DMRG, and how certain symmetries in the problem can be utilized to enhance its performance time for specific interesting cases.	As mentioned earlier, we leave for further research the question of how this algorithm can be used in combination with DMRG, and how certain symmetries in the problem can be utilized to enhance its performance time for specific interesting cases.	0.95
0912.0270	future_work	We propose several areas of improvement for the work presented in this thesis.	Part of the results presented in this thesis are published in {{cite:9ebf9e02288705b03074ebfed7da8465ca7af14e}}.  We propose several areas of improvement for the work presented in this thesis.	0.7
0912.1200	future_work	It is interesting to look at how to efficiently reduce time and message complexity by conducting edge contractions in parallel.	A synchronous distributed version of the Karger's randomized algorithm under network setting is presented in this paper with a proof of correctness and complexity analysis. The present algorithm appears not to make use of the full power of parallelism available. It is interesting to look at how to efficiently reduce time and message complexity by conducting edge contractions in parallel.	0.9
0912.2625	open_problem	In particular, it is not clear whether the existence of a large clique is decidable.	Our positive result Theorem REF  guarantees the existence of some clique or anticlique of size {{formula:6245c918-5e41-4a64-bba7-2750d3bb53bb}}  (and such a clique or anticlique can even be constructed). But the following situation is conceivable: the {{formula:f204aec9-36fa-4a1a-b7f8-4978ed7ba1dc}} -automatic graph contains large cliques without containing large cliques that can be described by a language from {{formula:193efa39-70aa-493a-95cc-888d114803bb}} . In particular, it is not clear whether the existence of a large clique is decidable.	0.9
0912.2625	open_problem	It is not clear whether this also holds for {{formula:87088fb6-b8bf-41e1-b899-533d35738a56}} -automatic graphs.	A related question concerns Ramsey quantifiers. Rubin {{cite:6db33a0f7cd99de03f15f2274c60f186f93e9151}} has shown that the set of nodes of an automatic graph whose neighbors contain an infinite anticlique is regular (his result is much more general, but this formulation suffices for our purpose). It is not clear whether this also holds for {{formula:87088fb6-b8bf-41e1-b899-533d35738a56}} -automatic graphs. A positive answer to this second question (assuming that it is effective) would entail an affirmative answer to the decidability question above.	0.9
0912.4184	future_work	In the future, we will extended our logic to deal with more programming language concepts: function calls, function pointers, class/object, generics, {{formula:91c4b1a0-a02f-47a7-aa0a-f966fd587257}} .	In the future, we will extended our logic to deal with more programming language concepts: function calls, function pointers, class/object, generics, {{formula:91c4b1a0-a02f-47a7-aa0a-f966fd587257}} . At the mean time, we will try to build a library of pre-defined DRFs, MSFs, and their properties for frequently used data structures.	0.9
0912.4184	future_work	At the mean time, we will try to build a library of pre-defined DRFs, MSFs, and their properties for frequently used data structures.	In the future, we will extended our logic to deal with more programming language concepts: function calls, function pointers, class/object, generics, {{formula:91c4b1a0-a02f-47a7-aa0a-f966fd587257}} . At the mean time, we will try to build a library of pre-defined DRFs, MSFs, and their properties for frequently used data structures.	0.8
0912.5353	future_work	Future work will investigate joint source-channel coding in multihop MIMO relay networks, extending the results of {{cite:ac49f050135083615acf7241bc2f5090e5948dc3}}.	We have studied the diversity-multiplexing-delay tradeoff in multihop MIMO networks by considering an optimal ARQ allocation problem to minimize the probability of error, which consists of the ARQ error and the packet loss due to queueing delay. Our contribution is two-fold: we combine the DMDT analysis with queueing network theory, and we use the tail probability of random delay to find the probability of packet loss due to queueing delay. Numerical results show that optimal ARQ should equalize the performance of each link and avoid long service times that cause large queueing delay. Future work will investigate joint source-channel coding in multihop MIMO relay networks, extending the results of {{cite:ac49f050135083615acf7241bc2f5090e5948dc3}}.	0.9
1001.0315	future_work	We leave those inconsistent results to future investigations.	Inputting the SDE solution of {{formula:7cdd0f00-f9c5-4edd-8aba-0d53cb49550d}} , we obtain numerical values and compare them with those we can find in literature. Some of them are consistent, some are not. We leave those inconsistent results to future investigations.	0.9
1001.0315	future_work	Another direction is to improve the precision of (REF ) and our ladder approximation SDE.	Based on them, one direction of the further research is to apply the order {{formula:bbf5aed9-d3be-48d2-b0ef-514d2c74dd1d}}  chiral Lagrangian to various pseudoscalar meson processes and discuss the corresponding physics. Another direction is to improve the precision of (REF ) and our ladder approximation SDE. With these improvements, we expect a more precise estimation on all LECs in future.	0.8
1001.1460	open_problem	If it is not height 1 we cannot say anything about that at the moment.	The above groups are intriguing: could it be the case that they are decomposable but not with Baumslag–Solitar groups? If this is the case then {{formula:8d5d873e-f253-4496-b9ea-4cd78dfa7965}} , where {{formula:28629747-c579-4f3e-a0b1-b45aed836668}}  will be two generator one relator group and the relator could be either of height 1 or not. One relator groups are well studied and all of the counter-examples seem to come from height 1 relators. Without loss of generality height one word is: {{formula:af8956f8-4495-4b09-825a-568d26c1ced9}}  If it is not height 1 we cannot say anything about that at the moment. But if {{formula:864e0420-0fa6-49e3-a93b-acdb664daea0}}  is then there is a theorem of J. Button in  which says that those groups are either large or are indistinguishable from Baumslag-Solitar groups from looking at subgroups. If {{formula:0fe842e7-a78b-4a98-9662-c9aa1047c47b}}  is large then certainly the {{formula:47d737ff-34e1-443c-b88e-bf018457f305}}  for some {{formula:52021fe8-9a45-4b75-b5a2-19c11fe01731}} . And also the above groups do not have the same number of subgroups as any Baumslag–Solitar groups.	0.9
1001.1460	open_problem	It maybe it might be the case that a higher index is needed to detect the required property.	It maybe it might be the case that a higher index is needed to detect the required property. Or do there exist doubles which have a surface subgroup but this is not detectable by Theorem  for arbitrary index? The property that the above groups seem to share is very little torsion in the abelianisation of subgroups. Also up to index 9 there is no abelianisation of a subgroup which has the repeating torsion, which could have been used to try the method described in Section 7.	0.8
1003.1682	future_work	A systematic study could be needed, investigating to what extent a domain specific language really is required to achieve the added benefit, or whether a well designed Python API (or API in any other programming language) would yield the same benefits.	The MSL ground control and observation software demonstrates an important concept: many critical systems already implement very powerful logging systems that can be used as a basis for automated evaluation of log files against requirements. Such log files can be analyzed with scripts (programs) written using a scripting (programming) language. However, there seems to be advantages to using a formal specification language, as demonstrated with this work. A systematic study could be needed, investigating to what extent a domain specific language really is required to achieve the added benefit, or whether a well designed Python API (or API in any other programming language) would yield the same benefits.	0.9
1003.3684	future_work	A rigorous study of the graphs generated by the proposed methods will reveal whether these methods can produce synthetic graphs with these properties.	There are other known and somewhat debatable properties of complex networks. A rigorous study of the graphs generated by the proposed methods will reveal whether these methods can produce synthetic graphs with these properties. This study will also provide us with better understanding of how the logics used in our algorithms affect the properties of the synthetic graphs they generate. Based on this study, we will develop a set of pre- and post-generation processing and randomization techniques that will enable us to construct a synthetic graph with desired properties.	0.9
1003.3684	future_work	Based on this study, we will develop a set of pre- and post-generation processing and randomization techniques that will enable us to construct a synthetic graph with desired properties.	There are other known and somewhat debatable properties of complex networks. A rigorous study of the graphs generated by the proposed methods will reveal whether these methods can produce synthetic graphs with these properties. This study will also provide us with better understanding of how the logics used in our algorithms affect the properties of the synthetic graphs they generate. Based on this study, we will develop a set of pre- and post-generation processing and randomization techniques that will enable us to construct a synthetic graph with desired properties.	0.8
1003.5621	open_problem	Is it true that any length space with Lebesgue's covering dimension \(d\) is a pro-Minkowski space of rank \(d\)?	5.1. Question.   Is it true that any length space with Lebesgue's covering dimension \(d\) is a pro-Minkowski space of rank \(d\) ?  Or even more specific:	0.9
1003.5621	open_problem	Is it true that any metric space which homeomorphic to a disk is a pro-Minkowski space of rank 2?	5.2. Question.   Is it true that any metric space which homeomorphic to a disk is a pro-Minkowski space of rank 2?  One can reformulate it philosophically: Is there any essential difference between Finsler metric and general metric on \(d\)-manifold? This question was asked by D. Burago; it was also original motivation for this paper (see also a related example \cite:4b3a40a9723f5f4c1782eb66258c556b4393109b).	0.9
1004.4402	future_work	As only undirected graphs are considered in this paper, it is worth further studying the directed FTNs based on trading direction, such as from buyer to seller, which reflects the money flow in the futures markets.	As only undirected graphs are considered in this paper, it is worth further studying the directed FTNs based on trading direction, such as from buyer to seller, which reflects the money flow in the futures markets. In Section REF , we simply established the weighted FTNs to demonstrate that active market participants get more active, but it is not enough. Actually, we can construct weighted FTNs or weighted and directed FTNs to investigate the market behaviors of active participants. Moreover, the generation model of FTNs is also an important issue for further exploration, as it can provide valuable insight on financial trade monitoring and risk control.	0.9
1004.4402	future_work	Moreover, the generation model of FTNs is also an important issue for further exploration, as it can provide valuable insight on financial trade monitoring and risk control.	As only undirected graphs are considered in this paper, it is worth further studying the directed FTNs based on trading direction, such as from buyer to seller, which reflects the money flow in the futures markets. In Section REF , we simply established the weighted FTNs to demonstrate that active market participants get more active, but it is not enough. Actually, we can construct weighted FTNs or weighted and directed FTNs to investigate the market behaviors of active participants. Moreover, the generation model of FTNs is also an important issue for further exploration, as it can provide valuable insight on financial trade monitoring and risk control.	0.85
1004.5186	future_work	development of a learning algorithm to improve the bandwidth \(b_0\) in (REF );	As future work we identify the following research directions that have the potential to improve ms-GMLogA:  development of a learning algorithm to improve the bandwidth \(b_0\)  in (REF );  development of a more sophisticated collective refinement of nodes;  development of a multiscale method for MLinGapA (defined below); and  more sophisticated multiscale organization of directed graphs.	0.9
1004.5186	future_work	A more sophisticated collective refinement of nodes can allow interruption of the coarsening earlier.	A more sophisticated collective refinement of nodes can allow interruption of the coarsening earlier. Thus, if some more qualitative approximation can be achieved at the very coarse levels instead of solving exactly the coarsest and applying fast simple refinement at the next few finer level, it can potentially lead to better solutions at the fine levels.	0.85
1005.0398	future_work	In future work, we will verify in detail our ability to resolve halo subclumps as well as measure their masses.	In this paper, we have focused on measurement of the radial mass profile of A1689. However, a key strength of LensPerfect is its ability to map massive substructure without assuming LTM. In future work, we will verify in detail our ability to resolve halo subclumps as well as measure their masses. The latter proves difficult (both in observed and simulated halos) as the subhalo masses must be disentangled cleanly from the greater parent halo , .	0.9
1005.0398	future_work	In future work we will develop algorithms to thoroughly explore the range of model solutions which perfectly reproduce all observed multiple image positions.	In future work we will develop algorithms to thoroughly explore the range of model solutions which perfectly reproduce all observed multiple image positions. This method must take care not to remain trapped in a local minimum near our “best” solution. Also, we must correctly account for the larger uncertainties in voids between the multiple images.	0.9
1005.2442	future_work	Future work will attempt at determining the complete statistics of the error covariance matrix of the Kalman Filter under Bernoulli losses.	In this paper we address the problem of state estimation for a discrete-time linear Gaussian system where observations are communicated to the estimator via a memoryless erasure channel. Following the work of Sinopoli et Al. {{cite:659136285851beffcfcc3de037a2dfdb1023c5e5}}, we were able to compute the value of the critical probability for a very general class of linear systems. The boundedness analysis in this paper can be easily generalized to general Markovian packet loss models and to the boundedness of higher moments of the error covariance. Future work will attempt at determining the complete statistics of the error covariance matrix of the Kalman Filter under Bernoulli losses.	0.9
1005.4752	future_work	experiences from relational database system design can be used to develop efficient implementations of such a system, possibly up to a point where applications run as fast as, or possibly even faster than, the dedicated prototypes we developed in the past.	The relationship between the region queries and the language modeling probability functions might seem trivial because we “hard-wired” the language modeling probability definition in the CONTAINING operator, but we believe it is remarkable: Note that the language modeling probability functions are arithmetic expressions that define the probability of a single document {{formula:a7ce3ed7-c8b0-40d6-a0b0-afcffd467a62}} . However, the region queries are algebraic expressions for processing sets of documents (regions) instead of single documents. Since the region query language forms a “bulk algebra”, experiences from relational database system design can be used to develop efficient implementations of such a system, possibly up to a point where applications run as fast as, or possibly even faster than, the dedicated prototypes we developed in the past.	0.9
1006.1944	open_problem	The effects are apparently due to the presence of material shells forming a sandwich on both sides of the motion plane; but the theoretical discussions are not yet concluded (see e.g. Zudov et al. \cite:181f75a61b460a8724ce408794e2eb62d3afcb2b).	The behavior of particle gas in {{formula:39600241-1ed6-4789-87f6-8b4cbe776897}}  in presence of the harmonic or biharmonic fields, shows a sequence of surprising reactions to the external electric force, in form of a 9giant \cite:3d9cc20364204ba346ab113635a467697c622f05 or vanishing, or even negative resistance \cite:f9d06506855fe5bb50aa85c446fd63c59faf2d27. The effects are apparently due to the presence of material shells forming a sandwich on both sides of the motion plane; but the theoretical discussions are not yet concluded (see e.g. Zudov et al. \cite:181f75a61b460a8724ce408794e2eb62d3afcb2b). One might henceforth ask, whether the surprising phenomena have not some elementary counterparts for time dependent fields? Could e.g., the behavior of the {{formula:3db7fc31-12b9-456a-9dea-a95956b2cf4d}}  evolution loops with the vanishing `fuzzy center', be interpreted as an analogue of an `anomalous resistance'? Could the distorted free evolution mean a modified effective time? Could the squeezing effects matter? Last not least, could the analogous phenomena affect the polarized vacuum, if such an entity exists \cite:876e49a2200a09f9e9704dbc1c346dc444d1b568?	0.9
1006.1944	open_problem	One might henceforth ask, whether the surprising phenomena have not some elementary counterparts for time dependent fields?	The behavior of particle gas in {{formula:39600241-1ed6-4789-87f6-8b4cbe776897}}  in presence of the harmonic or biharmonic fields, shows a sequence of surprising reactions to the external electric force, in form of a 9giant \cite:3d9cc20364204ba346ab113635a467697c622f05 or vanishing, or even negative resistance \cite:f9d06506855fe5bb50aa85c446fd63c59faf2d27. The effects are apparently due to the presence of material shells forming a sandwich on both sides of the motion plane; but the theoretical discussions are not yet concluded (see e.g. Zudov et al. \cite:181f75a61b460a8724ce408794e2eb62d3afcb2b). One might henceforth ask, whether the surprising phenomena have not some elementary counterparts for time dependent fields? Could e.g., the behavior of the {{formula:3db7fc31-12b9-456a-9dea-a95956b2cf4d}}  evolution loops with the vanishing `fuzzy center', be interpreted as an analogue of an `anomalous resistance'? Could the distorted free evolution mean a modified effective time? Could the squeezing effects matter? Last not least, could the analogous phenomena affect the polarized vacuum, if such an entity exists \cite:876e49a2200a09f9e9704dbc1c346dc444d1b568?	0.85
1006.4925	limitation	Until such time as there are formal techniques for evaluating social network simulations, we must rely on subjective evaluation, choosing the simulation model as sensibly as possible and allowing the results to speak for themselves.	In the past, ranking algorithms have tended to be evaluated only subjectively: if the algorithm produces subjectively accurate results, then it is an appropriate algorithm. The technique presented in this paper provides a measurable alternative. However, the question naturally arises of whether this technique itself is appropriate. How do we evaluate it? Until such time as there are formal techniques for evaluating social network simulations, we must rely on subjective evaluation, choosing the simulation model as sensibly as possible and allowing the results to speak for themselves. We have thoroughly described our simulation model and justified these choices wherever possible: our object model is based on the actor-concept-instance model of social-semantic networks, where change in the network is driven by an iterative process of user actions. Users are guided by metrics of cost, reward in conjunction with predefined metrics of quality as well as the ranking algorithms under investigation. We have presented the results of applying our technique to several common ranking algorithms, and we have shown that these results are reasonable.	0.9
1006.4925	future_work	In future, we intend to test our simulation model against multi-relational ranking algorithms which take advantage of the “semantics” of social-semantic networks.	In future, we intend to test our simulation model against multi-relational ranking algorithms which take advantage of the “semantics” of social-semantic networks. We plan to release our implementation software under an open-source license, so that it can be freely reused by developers of social networking applications.	0.95
1007.0920	future_work	As future work, we intend to use UDP for the initial measurements.	As future work, we intend to use UDP for the initial measurements. However, special care must be taken because a lower timing for UDP packages does not necessarily imply lower timings for TCP packages. A study must be made to determine the correspondence between UDP and TCP timings and how could UDP-based measurements be used to forecast the overhead introduced by TCP connections. This study must also take into consideration UDP packet losses that may also influence the total measurement time.	0.9
1007.1253	future_work	We show efficient recovery of vectors conforming to Zipfian or block sparse models, but leave open extending this to other models.	We show efficient recovery of vectors conforming to Zipfian or block sparse models, but leave open extending this to other models. Our framework decomposes the task into first locating the heavy hitters and then estimating them, and our set query algorithm is an efficient general solution for estimating the heavy hitters once found. The remaining task is to efficiently locate heavy hitters in other models.	0.9
1007.1253	open_problem	It would be valuable to reduce the independence needed, and hence the space required to store {{formula:5ed4158f-c829-4c55-b20a-0f81000a8ac2}} .	Our analysis assumes that the columns of {{formula:4c97487b-b2b4-4367-bcad-8318da3c31dd}}  are fully independent. It would be valuable to reduce the independence needed, and hence the space required to store {{formula:5ed4158f-c829-4c55-b20a-0f81000a8ac2}} .	0.8
1008.3977	limitation	In many fields, Citizendium does not meet our own standards – we do not have a full range of expert editors.	The project is still young, resulting in a number of challenges and opportunities. In many fields, Citizendium does not meet our own standards – we do not have a full range of expert editors. Larry Sanger once envisioned that Citizendium could reach 100,000 articles by 2012. This would, on average, require about 150 new articles a day to reach; the current level is around 15. It is not obvious how the necessary shift from linear to exponential growth can be achieved.	0.9
1008.3977	future_work	"We may need to explore how to codify our core policies and collaboration model as a possible MediaWiki extension, from which other wikis could possibly benefit – online, ""code is law"" {{cite:1021b9defdff6ee09420c2162f2b8745beb38964}}, as is currently being highlighted by sites like Stack Overflow which have changed the social interactions of participants by changing formal features of user experience and social structure."	"The project also needs to allow for more feedback by non-registered readers, without harming the currently very collegial atmosphere that is to a large extent due to the real-name policy and the respect for expertise. We may need to explore how to codify our core policies and collaboration model as a possible MediaWiki extension, from which other wikis could possibly benefit – online, ""code is law"" {{cite:1021b9defdff6ee09420c2162f2b8745beb38964}}, as is currently being highlighted by sites like Stack Overflow which have changed the social interactions of participants by changing formal features of user experience and social structure. We need to find financial backing and support. So far, the project has been run on a basically volunteer-only basis, yet the envisioned growth and improvement of English-language content and the possible start of branches in other languages require a higher degree of professionalisation, for which the upcoming Charter is meant as a basis."	0.85
1009.2123	future_work	An important future exercise, best conducted by IAEA safeguards experts, is a fuller analysis of the reactor safeguards implications of this novel bulk accountancy method.	The anomalous operations identified in this paper do not constitute a diversion scenario per se, since we have not specified the ultimate fate of the removed fuel. Instead, we have estimated the sensitivity of antineutrino rate measurements to changes in typical civil power reactor fuel loadings. An important future exercise, best conducted by IAEA safeguards experts, is a fuller analysis of the reactor safeguards implications of this novel bulk accountancy method.	0.9
1009.2123	future_work	To further examine the robustness of this method, it is necessary to investigate a wider class of anomalous scenarios, varying both fuel and reactor type.	More problematic in a safeguards context is the issue of deliberate misreporting of power levels on the part of the operator that would undermine the statistical power of our test. While this is a serious concern, we note that the operator's misreporting must be fully consistent with the antineutrino data, which are independently acquired by and remain under the control of the safeguards inspector. This independently acquired information places an important additional constraint on the operator compared to current practice, in which declarations, along with item accountancy, are the primary sources of quantitative information about the reactor thermal power and fuel loading. Moreover, the misrepresentation must be tuned to the particular anomalous operational state chosen by the operator. If different amounts or types of fissile material are removed, the hypothesis test may still detect a significant departure from the baseline. To further examine the robustness of this method, it is necessary to investigate a wider class of anomalous scenarios, varying both fuel and reactor type.	0.85
1010.1307	limitation	However, these datasets do not have very good redshift coverage.	The available {{formula:7a5298e6-45af-45ac-991d-be3c00c5f2ca}}  data have so far proven to be a useful tool in the pursuit of understanding the expansion history of the universe and the possible nature of dark energy. However, these datasets do not have very good redshift coverage. The current measurements have gone as deep as {{formula:58fa6438-0c8d-4ea6-95f7-732e938ebc23}} , and this redshift range is only sparsely covered. There is also another issue of the large error bars associated with the {{formula:5930d9bc-60e4-40cc-a89c-5dad5bdac86d}}  figures from the differential age method. On the other hand, the collection of more and higher quality {{formula:c0e979cb-b91a-4591-a339-d79a947e22ba}}  data will not only help us constrain the parameters, but will also allow us to understand the possible tension between {{formula:9a40676b-898f-4b64-ac80-3a9de24f4ba2}}  and other cosmological tests. The latter is important, because tension is usually an indicator of systematic errors in the data. By understanding the tension, we may finally conquer the systematic effects that have not yet been modelled well enough.	0.9
1010.1307	future_work	By understanding the tension, we may finally conquer the systematic effects that have not yet been modelled well enough.	The available {{formula:7a5298e6-45af-45ac-991d-be3c00c5f2ca}}  data have so far proven to be a useful tool in the pursuit of understanding the expansion history of the universe and the possible nature of dark energy. However, these datasets do not have very good redshift coverage. The current measurements have gone as deep as {{formula:58fa6438-0c8d-4ea6-95f7-732e938ebc23}} , and this redshift range is only sparsely covered. There is also another issue of the large error bars associated with the {{formula:5930d9bc-60e4-40cc-a89c-5dad5bdac86d}}  figures from the differential age method. On the other hand, the collection of more and higher quality {{formula:c0e979cb-b91a-4591-a339-d79a947e22ba}}  data will not only help us constrain the parameters, but will also allow us to understand the possible tension between {{formula:9a40676b-898f-4b64-ac80-3a9de24f4ba2}}  and other cosmological tests. The latter is important, because tension is usually an indicator of systematic errors in the data. By understanding the tension, we may finally conquer the systematic effects that have not yet been modelled well enough.	0.85
1010.2511	future_work	This includes improvements to the code base of MARFCAT as well as resolving unfinished scenarios and results, addressing shortcomings in Section REF , testing more algorithms and combinations from the related work, and moving onto other programming languages (e.g. PHP, ASP, C#).	There is a great number of possibilities in the future work. This includes improvements to the code base of MARFCAT as well as resolving unfinished scenarios and results, addressing shortcomings in Section REF , testing more algorithms and combinations from the related work, and moving onto other programming languages (e.g. PHP, ASP, C#).	0.9
1010.2511	future_work	Furthermore, plan to conceive collaboration with vendors such as VeraCode, Coverity, and others who have vast data sets to test the full potential of the approach with the others and a community as a whole.	There is a great number of possibilities in the future work. This includes improvements to the code base of MARFCAT as well as resolving unfinished scenarios and results, addressing shortcomings in Section REF , testing more algorithms and combinations from the related work, and moving onto other programming languages (e.g. PHP, ASP, C#). Furthermore, plan to conceive collaboration with vendors such as VeraCode, Coverity, and others who have vast data sets to test the full potential of the approach with the others and a community as a whole. Then move on to dynamic code analysis as well applying similar techniques there.	0.8
1010.2783	future_work	In this new setting our {{formula:9a44a00c-9ef2-4e86-abb5-878e47d80899}}  parameter start to play a rôle in the virus as well as antidote spreading on the network. In the future we will study this problem.	As we have exposed in the sections corresponding to our proposal, if under our model {{formula:c60bb909-01f3-4cc2-84b6-4f545138b193}} , our fix point and fast extinction condition are consistent with those obtained in {{cite:c18a657151d844e2317354475a031b4c41475f1b}}. In the other side, if under our model {{formula:4e417787-4681-46bd-8f9a-c31700a4dbe1}} , then the fix point and fast extinction condition mentioned in the appendix section of {{cite:c18a657151d844e2317354475a031b4c41475f1b}} are not longer valid. In this last case the dynamical system becomes nonlinear, and the degree of non-linearity will depend on the topology of the network. In this new setting our {{formula:9a44a00c-9ef2-4e86-abb5-878e47d80899}}  parameter start to play a rôle in the virus as well as antidote spreading on the network. In the future we will study this problem.	0.9
1010.2783	future_work	If we take as starting point this scenario it can be interesting to ask if the system falls in a chaotic regime and if this is the case then how the stability of the network can be re-established.	If we take as starting point this scenario it can be interesting to ask if the system falls in a chaotic regime and if this is the case then how the stability of the network can be re-established. If we want to achieve this state of the system we can recall the synchronization and chaos tools developed in the research field of automatic control. This is one of the subjects that we will try to explore in the future.	0.95
1011.3603	future_work	Along this direction, higher-order correlation function can also be considered.	The scattering matrix method is initiated by Landauer and Büttiker to investigate the conductance of multi-terminal and multi-channel mesoscopic conductors. The spin degree of freedom can be included in the formalism by enlargement of the dimension of the scattering matrix. The current-current correlation and spin-spin correlation, such as the shot noise, can be calculated from the cross products of the scattering matrix. Along this direction, higher-order correlation function can also be considered. Dynamic transport processes including the quantum pumping behavior can be dealt with by the time-dependent scattering approach.	0.9
1011.3603	future_work	Some particular problems include non-harmonically driven quantum pumping, spin pumping in racetrack memory applications, and multiferroic transport dynamics, etc..	The development of the scattering theory enables its potential applications in currently open issues. The interaction can be included to the scattering matrix by the renormalization factors. The shot noise properties in various conductors with active spin degree of freedom can be considered. The time-dependent scattering theory provides a way to deal with dynamic quantum issues. Some particular problems include non-harmonically driven quantum pumping, spin pumping in racetrack memory applications, and multiferroic transport dynamics, etc..	0.95
1011.6516	future_work	Its role fully remains to be investigated.	First, the role of several parameters still remains to be assessed: the mean temperature {{formula:831c3283-e7d6-4d3d-9f75-18b5a051d311}}  around which the variations of amplitude {{formula:f7a4b07b-72ad-4c26-9a61-9800e726b4b9}}  are imposed might play a role on the reorganization process inside the pile. Because of the presence of the container and the fact that the granular pile is a deformable medium, the mean temperature impacts on the initial stress distribution and on the space available for the grains. Its role fully remains to be investigated. Also, the parameters relevant in the case of vibrated grains are to be studied here. Namely, the shape of the grains {{cite:061627e749b7515eab8c63f36054711988dcf6cf}}, {{cite:faea02c71342f129ff7494fd6007b1d4c3f0ffb9}}, {{cite:511ef4658e98fcf4fc04447283b3722a28ded40c}}, {{cite:29930a6144c4caf0184e77e1d6b9fc49e393e9b8}}, their surface roughness {{cite:a0b41411e2ad826f27d407aab5bc93cfebfab5a4}}, {{cite:59e08f176ba696a37778277dea94c40b3ec99afa}}, their polydispersity {{cite:25b38f5ad8a9c7ad43563b0bcea7011521104fea}}, etc. But also, the size ratio of the grains and the container {{cite:c8fdc7cdbf5491c7dc10aae00b7fe8382c260442}}. Such a study might help to compare more accurately tapping and thermal cycling experiments. It could then be relevant to mix the two types of solicitation on a granular assembly to test the statistical framework proposed by Edwards {{cite:4b20955d8f8b3de6051c574ce082c66587ce5183}}, {{cite:1f13eb9a1c2e82b7557f5fd7cd4726a477ee83c5}}. Indeed, recent experimental results lead to think that the steady state packing fraction reached by a shaken granular column (and function of {{formula:31b25587-a7bb-4138-838d-2a435ddbfe61}} ) is a genuine thermodynamic state, within this theoretical framework {{cite:e016d2d9fcf129ba71c2ac987b2d4d8e1d5e73b1}}, {{cite:947a6abf1389096760306ad775cbb3a3f3a49516}}. It might thus be relevant to study the fluctuations of packing fraction {{cite:8ca1af47aff85388a061a91419249f20dffa68cf}} around its equilibrium value, induced by cycles of temperature. Also, moisture is another relevant parameter that would deserve an exhaustive experimental study. Indeed, the existence of a liquid bridge between two adjacent grains is expected to increase the effective thermal conductivity {{cite:78e98ef40141743f2093e15a4a3df8ebb9c282c3}} and thus to reinforce the role of the force chains inside the pile.	0.9
1011.6516	future_work	Second, the aging phenomenon observed under thermal cycling needs to be better characterized.	Second, the aging phenomenon observed under thermal cycling needs to be better characterized. In particular, how can we compare the aging effects observed in vibrated granular piles and described in {{cite:12da63c778fee31e3d35a861eb72b9dd5f81246f}} to the one described here ? Another way to put it would be to ask if we can loosen a sample by means of cycles of temperature.	0.85
1011.6627	open_problem	How to obtain the best set of weights remains an open problem and definitely deserves further investigations.	Although the expression (REF ) provides access to exact statistics for a broader domain of problems and our expansion formula (REF ) provides accurate and stable statistics even when nearly degenerate weights are present, there remain a few unanswered questions that should be addressed by the community in the near future. For example, even though we can accommodate any reasonable {{formula:b57b8d50-ab05-41ad-a853-6c382dbac592}} -value weighting, thanks to (REF ), the more difficult question is how does one choose the right set of weights when combining statistical significance {{cite:e139a7d8e070a0863f11f0f70e4809a7866fd5b7}}, {{cite:49ac8ee12bc4f442c13afd78124e4e6885981200}}, {{cite:bb3f271ccecbf98e0239cbf9efd30cee44b7ae5e}}, {{cite:c47afd007bcde91812f4a998ea80357b296d7772}}, {{cite:5fb17e9c7af7a8c5ceb5fdb14992bf329ed7cf97}}. The weights chosen reflects how much does one wish to trust various obtained {{formula:2f8718dd-6c31-4cd5-ac27-60edf1a06144}} -values. Ideally, a fully systematic method should also provide a metric for choosing appropriate weights. How to obtain the best set of weights remains an open problem and definitely deserves further investigations.	0.95
1011.6627	open_problem	How to obtain the correlation {{cite:6c1db2c8685bb166f4c2f72377c8095370aa5412}}, {{cite:825e628c5136b809d4be55a9622b7831ae5ac2ff}}, {{cite:31150aaf62acabe8dc82ef1f48714f23e17d6c85}} and properly take into account the existence of {{formula:c23c02a9-5dd9-49e0-853b-a37a4a4de6e9}} -value correlations {{cite:1027cb06843632fe82207acc730f82166c0bd708}}, {{cite:dd65810bda95acc6d833ee31a14ae7fad267e834}}, {{cite:1d103707f3f812f52b2285b7222beeb594586881}} is also a challenging problem that we hope to address in the near future.	Another limitation of (REF ) and (REF ), and consequently of Fisher's and Good's formulas, is that one must assume the {{formula:527823c8-79d2-44ca-8e2a-5ec7a78382e0}} -values to be combined as independent. In real applications, it is foreseeable that {{formula:7949f3c0-32a6-4a91-a6af-f662f430de0a}} -values reported by various methods may exhibit non-negligible correlations. How to obtain the correlation {{cite:6c1db2c8685bb166f4c2f72377c8095370aa5412}}, {{cite:825e628c5136b809d4be55a9622b7831ae5ac2ff}}, {{cite:31150aaf62acabe8dc82ef1f48714f23e17d6c85}} and properly take into account the existence of {{formula:c23c02a9-5dd9-49e0-853b-a37a4a4de6e9}} -value correlations {{cite:1027cb06843632fe82207acc730f82166c0bd708}}, {{cite:dd65810bda95acc6d833ee31a14ae7fad267e834}}, {{cite:1d103707f3f812f52b2285b7222beeb594586881}} is also a challenging problem that we hope to address in the near future.	0.9
1012.0821	open_problem	Perhaps the most important open problem related to our work is the complexity of {{formula:ea845fe2-dbaf-4c12-88e7-59d0d08b67f5}} -turn interactive proofs with competing provers for constants {{formula:8518a310-f581-4477-906c-7aa570667ce2}} .	More than two turns, only one prover per team. Perhaps the most important open problem related to our work is the complexity of {{formula:ea845fe2-dbaf-4c12-88e7-59d0d08b67f5}} -turn interactive proofs with competing provers for constants {{formula:8518a310-f581-4477-906c-7aa570667ce2}} . This problem, which dates back at least to 1997 {{cite:5e1ea1125597d0f41f110e7a305419d6bd996a55}}, is still open even in the special case of only one prover per team. With only one prover per team, the question is really a game-theoretic question with a much wider application than just interactive proofs. Our method fails for this case because we do not have a bound on the verifier matrix of the form {{formula:c29f8079-8966-4b1c-9b8b-321e9239ea63}}  such as that appearing in Proposition REF . Thus, we do not obtain a good enough bound on the loss vectors appearing in our variant of the multiplicative weights update method.	0.9
1012.0821	open_problem	Even with two no-signaling provers, two turns of interaction, and only one team of provers, it is still not known that the {{formula:e39348a3-ac23-4dca-a413-1a30f559de65}}  upper bound holds when either the verifier or provers can send quantum messages {{cite:f468890068ec1525365ff1246672e9674ec005c5}}.	Quantum verifier and/or provers. Even with two no-signaling provers, two turns of interaction, and only one team of provers, it is still not known that the {{formula:e39348a3-ac23-4dca-a413-1a30f559de65}}  upper bound holds when either the verifier or provers can send quantum messages {{cite:f468890068ec1525365ff1246672e9674ec005c5}}. Here the problem is that Lemma REF  does not hold for quantum states.	0.85
