1105.2968	future_work	Finding the correct goodness of fit statistics measuring the distance between the real consumer finance portfolio and RCFDG.	The next steps probably would be concentrated on:  Finding the correct goodness of fit statistics measuring the distance between the real consumer finance portfolio and RCFDG. Also it should be tested the property of that statistics.	0.9
1105.2968	future_work	Creating more general case with all collection processes, more than one credit per customer, more than one macro-economic factors and other detailed issues.	The next steps probably would be concentrated on:  Finding the correct goodness of fit statistics measuring the distance between the real consumer finance portfolio and RCFDG. Also it should be tested the property of that statistics.  Analyzing the additional constraints to satisfy for example properties like: the predictive power, measured for example by Gini {{cite:a8ddccc5cbb3f5771afebde558962b1c86893484}}, of characteristic {{formula:450ff615-6827-4e01-81b7-f5862952fbd2}}  on {{formula:7e91b929-9ec8-4666-8bd9-21e08c65c084}}  should be equaled to {{formula:4aa2a749-0a13-44a0-89c5-ce0e8f44a6db}} .  Creating more general case with all collection processes, more than one credit per customer, more than one macro-economic factors and other detailed issues.	0.85
1105.4058	limitation	most of them share the problem that the evaluation is carried on over small databases, making the results obtained difficult to generalize.	So far, the academic community has produced several works on this topic, but most of them share the problem that the evaluation is carried on over small databases, making the results obtained difficult to generalize.	0.9
1105.4058	future_work	Next, the mid-term and long-term reliability of heart sounds will be assessed, analyzing how their biometric properties change as time goes by.	Next, the mid-term and long-term reliability of heart sounds will be assessed, analyzing how their biometric properties change as time goes by. Additionally, the impact of cardiac diseases on the identification performance will be assessed.	0.8
1105.5509	limitation	However, the techniques we have developed here are somewhat sensitive to the distribution of workload over the grading lattice: if certain degrees are disproportionately densely populated, then the computational burden of an entire GrÃ¶bner basis is dictated by the essentially serial computation of the highly populated degrees.	However, the techniques we have developed here are somewhat sensitive to the distribution of workload over the grading lattice: if certain degrees are disproportionately densely populated, then the computational burden of an entire GrÃ¶bner basis is dictated by the essentially serial computation of the highly populated degrees. As such, we suspect these methods to work at their very best in combination with other parallelization techniques.	0.9
1105.5509	future_work	The GrÃ¶bner basis implementation used was a rather naÃ¯ve one, and we fully expect speed-ups from sophisticated algorithms to combine cleanly with the constructions we use.	The GrÃ¶bner basis implementation used was a rather naÃ¯ve one, and we fully expect speed-ups from sophisticated algorithms to combine cleanly with the constructions we use. This is something we expect to examine in future continuation of this project.	0.9
1106.0776	future_work	One of the main topics to explore is to show that the possibilistic answer set semantics can be characterized as a logic inference in terms of a possibilistic version of the intuitionistic logic.	In future work, there are several topics which will be explored. One of the main topics to explore is to show that the possibilistic answer set semantics can be characterized as a logic inference in terms of a possibilistic version of the intuitionistic logic. This issue is motivated by the fact that the answer set semantic inference can be characterized as a logic inference in terms of intuitionistic logic {{cite:6612d077dedc6b6f12a484bd037208c162adc26b}}, {{cite:aa27d02f6059d2ba2db28fa4a9295c8bf7331690}}.	0.9
1106.0776	future_work	On the other hand, we have been exploring to define a possibilistic action language.	In future work, there are several topics which will be explored. One of the main topics to explore is to show that the possibilistic answer set semantics can be characterized as a logic inference in terms of a possibilistic version of the intuitionistic logic. This issue is motivated by the fact that the answer set semantic inference can be characterized as a logic inference in terms of intuitionistic logic {{cite:6612d077dedc6b6f12a484bd037208c162adc26b}}, {{cite:aa27d02f6059d2ba2db28fa4a9295c8bf7331690}}. On the other hand, we have been exploring to define a possibilistic action language. In {{cite:fedd8e5bfd16db343361b286bfbf0790eda485fa}}, we have already defined our first ideas in the context of the action language {{formula:1c4d872e-9e3a-4762-ac07-cae7ae739cf7}} . Finally, we have started to explore the definition of a possibilistic framework in order to define preference between rules and preferences between atoms. With this objective in mind, possibilistic ordered disjunction programs have been explored {{cite:b36366a807e1c63d3826c8aee945b5ca7b70c108}}.	0.8
1106.2440	future_work	Clearly investigating the mechanism design problem for additional graph properties, such as the clustering coefficient is of interest.	There are several directions this research could take. In {{cite:4398bc01205ed93640c44bbf71c356bb9ee094cd}} we begin the investigation of graph formation games with specific link bias and identify a game theoretic mechanism that does not explicitly encode the degree distribution of interest into the players' objective functions and yet is still capable of having graphs with arbitrary degree distributions as stable solutions. This work is extended in {{cite:c6b4787101bbb48ba921dd6c5df6228c471e6bfb}}. We also investigate the graph formation game in the presence of spatial oligopolies in {{cite:d35209b23e087784ba8d051dc74644123b60c5d0}}. Clearly investigating the mechanism design problem for additional graph properties, such as the clustering coefficient is of interest. Recent work on generation of graphs with specific clustering coefficients {{cite:30f7b20edc49f5761b6c43b3ef4e83a29c8a0688}} may provide insight into game theoretic mechanisms for solving the same problem.	0.9
1106.2440	future_work	Another equally interesting future direction lies in the extraction of game theoretic mechanisms from real-world data.	Another equally interesting future direction lies in the extraction of game theoretic mechanisms from real-world data. Solving such a problem may take two forms. In the first form, small real world human networks are evaluated using traditional psycho-social interviewing techniques in an attempt to identify objective functions or constraints consistent with the model presented in this paper and theoretical extensions (e.g., {{cite:c6b4787101bbb48ba921dd6c5df6228c471e6bfb}}). This work is planned in collaboration with social psychologists at the authors' parent institution. A second form of objective function inference would be completely observational in which, given a network that appeared to be stable, one would attempt to infer the set of potential objective functions (and player constraints {{cite:4398bc01205ed93640c44bbf71c356bb9ee094cd}}) that would generate the given stable graph. Observation of network evolution would also be useful. In this case, statistical techniques would have to be applied to the determination of the objective function structure.	0.95
1106.3478	future_work	While the “how” of CECD is fully understood, the question of “where” and “when”, i.e. coming up with good heuristics for the selection of the conditional and region of duplication, needs much further investigation.	While the “how” of CECD is fully understood, the question of “where” and “when”, i.e. coming up with good heuristics for the selection of the conditional and region of duplication, needs much further investigation. Also, experiments with real code have yet to be conducted to quantify the benefit and suggest good values for the heuristics’ parameters. Another possible improvement would be to not only consider syntactically equal conditions, but also take algebraic identities into account.	0.9
1106.3478	future_work	Also, experiments with real code have yet to be conducted to quantify the benefit and suggest good values for the heuristics’ parameters.	While the “how” of CECD is fully understood, the question of “where” and “when”, i.e. coming up with good heuristics for the selection of the conditional and region of duplication, needs much further investigation. Also, experiments with real code have yet to be conducted to quantify the benefit and suggest good values for the heuristics’ parameters. Another possible improvement would be to not only consider syntactically equal conditions, but also take algebraic identities into account.	0.8
1106.3736	open_problem	Is it true that any \(k\)-convex open subset of \(\mathbb{E}^m\) is Lefschetz-\(k\)-convex?	It is easy to show that Lefschetz-\(k\)-convexity in \(\mathbb{E}^m\) implies our \(k\)-convexity. We know that the converse holds in two trivial cases: \(k=1\) and \(k=m-1\), but in all other cases we do not know the answer to the following question.  Open problem Is it true that any \(k\)-convex open subset of \(\mathbb{E}^m\) is Lefschetz-\(k\)-convex?	0.95
1106.3736	open_problem	Is it possible to construct a Riemannian metric \(g\) on the product of a torus and an open disc \(D^2\) such that the torus \(T^2\) is flat and the curvature is strictly positive outside of a compact set?	The following question of D. Burago and B. Kleiner is open for long time. It is not directly relevant to all above, but it was one of the initial motivations for our work.  Question Is it possible to construct a Riemannian metric \(g\) on the product of a torus and an open disc \(D^2\)  such that the torus \(T^2\) is flat and the curvature is strictly positive outside of a compact set?	0.9
1106.5178	future_work	The OAC specification is currently in Alpha3 stage and our future work will focus on the following issues: support for structured bodies that go beyond resource referencing and string-literal representation, extension mechanisms for addressing complex media segments in various media types, and processing of constraints.	The OAC specification is currently in Alpha3 stage and our future work will focus on the following issues: support for structured bodies that go beyond resource referencing and string-literal representation, extension mechanisms for addressing complex media segments in various media types, and processing of constraints.	0.9
1106.5178	future_work	We will further pursue the integration of the OAC segment identification approach with the W3C Media Fragment Identification mechanism.	We will further pursue the integration of the OAC segment identification approach with the W3C Media Fragment Identification mechanism. Since it is hardly possible to address all possible segment shapes in a fragment identification specification, we propose an additional fragment key/value pair for the spatial dimension, which enables fragment identification by reference in W3C Media Fragment URIs. The key could be ptr, ref or something similar and the value a URI. The URI points to a resource, which provides further information about the properties of the spatial region/segment. We suggested this to the Media Fragment Working Group and hope that this issue will be addressed in future W3C Media Fragments URI recommendations.	0.85
1107.3226	future_work	A very interesting question is: can we find a physical mechanism as the fastest implementation of an arbitrary functions?	This is a exciting field, we believe there is more exciting topic to discussed. A very interesting question is: can we find a physical mechanism as the fastest implementation of an arbitrary functions?	0.9
1107.3226	future_work	We shall try to work on this in the future.	In Sec-IV, we talked about quantum simulations and how to construct a clever problem to induced a quantum algorithm. Actually, we conjecture that the problem we construct is a hard one in class {{formula:a4ffb1ec-bfe9-49ce-9889-5d7a12b093f2}} , for these questions have a counting style. However, we are not sure about whether the designed questions could be in {{formula:484a8510-2c32-4cd2-ab0c-9979036c2f90}}  under some specific statistical models. We shall try to work on this in the future.	0.8
1107.4661	future_work	Fix grammar fragments.	Naturally, this effort is one step in a long way, and we take the rest of the report to sketch the next milestones and planned deliverables:  Fix grammar fragments. The first thing we can do is regenerate the original grammar fragments in the same notation. One one hand, this would help to not alienate the grammar from its creators; on the other hand, the fragments will use a consistent notation throughout the grammar and be validated as not having any misspellings, metasymbol omissions, etc.	0.9
1107.4661	future_work	Find ambiguities and other problems.	Find ambiguities and other problems. Various grammar analysis techniques referenced in the text above can be used to perform deeper analyses on the grammar in order to make it fully operational in Rascal, resolve existing ambiguities, and perhaps even spot problems that are unavoidable with the current notation.	0.85
1107.5474	limitation	The model is concerned with association rule reasoning and it does not use -in its current form- more sophisticated probability tools (as for example {{cite:6e0f0809cbe021362cf00efcbe3e150026a6fa1c}}).	The selection attribute problem based on FCA-based reasoning system for sport forecasting is analysed. In fact, the reasoning system is a computational logic model for bounded rationality. The model is concerned with association rule reasoning and it does not use -in its current form- more sophisticated probability tools (as for example {{cite:6e0f0809cbe021362cf00efcbe3e150026a6fa1c}}). As is stated in {{cite:b2770b7479ca355b3c35e5051c8e10150cd6755a}}, the theory of probabilistic mental models assumes that inferences about unknown states of the world are based on probability cues {{cite:1d0a69e802ca05ec11cf5d6a5f6660dffb7d52dd}}. It can say that confidence of association rules extracted from subcontexts play the role of probability cues.	0.9
1107.5474	future_work	Future works pass through for finding these complementary attribute sets and detecting when their behaviors change during season in order to select the proper attribute set to forecast each week.	After some real betting experiments during current season (2010-2011) with one customized attribute set, we have observed another intriguing fact. If we take a look to number of successful predictions per week, we are able to distinguish some groups of consecutive weeks in which number of correct predictions is under or over the average. Recall that these predictions are the logical inferred results by one customized attribute set. This suggests that it could be possible to find another attribute set, with a different parameters customization, which it will accomplish the correct predictions of first attribute set. It means that when first attribute set produce bad forecasting, second should produce good ones, and vice versa. The reason of this is that each match there is not only one possible logical result. It means, when one of firsts teams of current ranking plays against one of lasts team, attending to ranking criteria, the logical result of this match would be that first one wins. But if we attend to others, like first team lost last week and second team won last 5 weeks, this results would be different. Future works pass through for finding these complementary attribute sets and detecting when their behaviors change during season in order to select the proper attribute set to forecast each week.	0.95
1108.3915	future_work	The next step would be to improve the prototype and perform more comprehensive evaluations.	We have implemented a simple prototype and carried out preliminary evaluation of our framework. The next step would be to improve the prototype and perform more comprehensive evaluations. More specifically, the cloud-like environment set up in the experiment contains only two data servers. In addition, only one dataset comes from real monitoring stations, and the workloads are synthetic. Therefore, we plan to acquire more realistic datasets and workloads, and to evaluate the prototype with larger numbers of data servers. We also plan to export our prototype into real cloud environments such as Amazon EC2 and Microsoft's Azure {{cite:0a42b4dd6054485776a743d0abc1ea5031d97f30}}, {{cite:7430927379a3a4562002db7dcdf650954c250a23}}, and benchmark it with real data mining applications accessing real datasets.	0.9
1108.3915	future_work	As shown in Table REF , eXACML only deals with archival databases and queries.	As shown in Table REF , eXACML only deals with archival databases and queries. The immediate extension will be to support stream databases and continuous queries. Relational databases are not the best tool for handling stream data, for which other models have been proposed {{cite:b130dd7406f253f3c77b02dc10bcfde79cc55148}}. We will examine the design and compare performance of the extended eXACML to that of the existing works on access control for stream data {{cite:e310c65bedd5ef20f9291e03aca5c66daf9d543f}}, {{cite:3d46e0f66fe7fc9671c6f95a95ac674896b31360}}.	0.85
1108.5985	future_work	One algorithm that should be experimentally evaluated is the following.	One algorithm that should be experimentally evaluated is the following. We perform a search over the vertices of {{formula:7143b7f3-fce8-49b6-bbe1-b3f73f071f64}} , that is, we build a search tree with flips as edges. We keep a set with the extreme vertices with respect to a given projection. Each computed vertex that is not extreme in the above set is discarded and no flips are executed on it, i.e. the search tree is pruned in this vertex. The search procedure could be the algorithm of TOPCOM or the one presented in Ref. {{cite:b68fe42c2ae8da5785ac966b64ee0621039d191b}} which builds a search tree in some equivalence classes of {{formula:111fa37f-ec19-487e-a1d8-7f39cc08d698}} . The main advantage of this algorithm is that it does not involve a convex hull computation. On the other hand, it is not output-sensitive with respect to the number of vertices of the resultant polytope; its complexity depends on the number of vertices on the silhouette of {{formula:1f8f2b14-1b5d-4e70-867d-5bdbf283723e}} , with respect to a given projection and those that are connected by an edge with them.	0.9
1108.5985	open_problem	The basic open question that this paper raises is whether there is a polynomial total time algorithm for {{formula:439ccf85-5805-408f-8147-024e7cb75400}}  or even for the set of its vertices.	Our proposed algorithm uses an incremental convex hull algorithm and it is known that any such algorithm has a worst-case super-polynomial total time complexity {{cite:ece43dff68b2d483ee4738fba1542d081c2ba783}} in the number of input points and output facets. The basic open question that this paper raises is whether there is a polynomial total time algorithm for {{formula:439ccf85-5805-408f-8147-024e7cb75400}}  or even for the set of its vertices.	1.0
1109.0217	future_work	Moreover, one can use different tight-frame systems such as those from contourlets, curvelets or steerable-wavelet to capture more directions along the boundary.	The method can be implemented fast and give very accurate, smooth boundaries or surfaces. In addition, since the pixel values of more and more pixels will be set to either 0 or 1 during the iterations, by taking advantage of this, one can construct a sparse data structure to accelerate the method. Moreover, one can use different tight-frame systems such as those from contourlets, curvelets or steerable-wavelet {{cite:8ff8202cf06b7348d6d72420dfb0bf22fe632453}}, {{cite:f28c2661ddecc60ae1e2dbed090d143b09c5e77f}}, {{cite:0f95a0fb0e378d8f648bc1c49c5b6a13e4fc9731}} to capture more directions along the boundary.	0.9
1109.0217	future_work	Though we have proved that our algorithm will always converge to a binary image, it will be interesting to see what functional the binary image is minimizing.	Though we have proved that our algorithm will always converge to a binary image, it will be interesting to see what functional the binary image is minimizing. The framework for proving convergence for tight-frame algorithms, as developed in {{cite:c3921281419bec53d71201994bd4ffdeef21c52f}}, may be useful here. These are the directions we will explore in the future.	0.95
1109.0470	open_problem	Quantum Nambu Dynamics of Surfaces raises also the issue of possible existence of Quantum Strange Attractors.	Quantum Nambu Dynamics of Surfaces {{cite:e766d4ae42b21a0e88cf5e51ecf775230c416db1}}, {{cite:8ab241813224c21816764204736d71ae2da4f24c}}, {{cite:13c79b325ea2474cfe399568370c01dd4c3dd82b}}, raises also the issue of possible existence of Quantum Strange Attractors.	0.9
1111.1497	future_work	In such a scenario, an IR-based evaluation scheme gains relevance because validation against a fixed set of gold standard segmentation may often lead to overfitting of the algorithms without yielding any real benefit.	Moreover, we gain several useful and non-intuitive insights from the evaluation experiments. Most importantly, we show that human notions of query segments may not be the best for maximizing retrieval performance, and treating them as the gold standard limits the scope for improvement for an algorithm. Also, the matching metrics extensively used till date for comparing against gold standard segmentations can often be misleading. We would like to emphasize that in the future, the focus of IR will mostly shift to tail queries. In such a scenario, an IR-based evaluation scheme gains relevance because validation against a fixed set of gold standard segmentation may often lead to overfitting of the algorithms without yielding any real benefit.	0.9
1111.1497	future_work	A hypothetical oracle has been shown to be quite useful, but we realize that it will be a much bigger contribution to the community if we could implement a context-aware oracle that can actually tell the search engine which version of a segmented query should be chosen at runtime.	A hypothetical oracle has been shown to be quite useful, but we realize that it will be a much bigger contribution to the community if we could implement a context-aware oracle that can actually tell the search engine which version of a segmented query should be chosen at runtime.	0.95
1111.3083	limitation	However, the model developed for the radial evolution of dust grains in this paper remains simple in that we neglect several important physical processes: turbulence, grain growth, collective motion of dust grains, dust feedback on the gas surface density and temperature profiles.	We have demonstrated that the time it takes for grains to reach smaller and smaller radii increases dramatically under certain conditions. Specifically, if {{formula:cf54e83b-bc66-4643-acc7-301566eb8655}}  (resp. {{formula:d12f18ad-83d8-49ab-b8bc-8640910dee56}} ), grains experience a pile-up in the Epstein (resp. Stokes) regime. However, the model developed for the radial evolution of dust grains in this paper remains simple in that we neglect several important physical processes: turbulence, grain growth, collective motion of dust grains, dust feedback on the gas surface density and temperature profiles. We now discuss how those processes can modify the criteria derived above.	0.9
1111.3083	future_work	Full simulations like those developed in {{cite:63517d4fa233f717ba6823e48bbe9270fac5ec5a}} are required to make predictions of the dust kinetics, even more so when the grain size evolution is driven by a complex model of growth and fragmentation.	It should be noted that the criterion {{formula:a4ec6d10-9a40-46d3-92b5-0e61bfb0fea3}}  quantifies the outcome of the radial-drift motion of the grains, but not their kinematics (which depends on the grain size, the grain density, etc...). Thus, we provide predictions for which discs will retain the largest mass of solid particles, but do not predict for which discs the radial migration to the inner disc regions is the fastest. Full simulations like those developed in {{cite:63517d4fa233f717ba6823e48bbe9270fac5ec5a}} are required to make predictions of the dust kinetics, even more so when the grain size evolution is driven by a complex model of growth and fragmentation. However, this study suggests that while complex simulations are useful to study the details of the dust dynamics, they are not required to determine the grains outcome.	0.85
1112.0343	future_work	We plan to investigate rewriting and optimization techniques for sticky-join sets of TGDs, and alternative forms of rewriting such as positive-existential queries.	We plan to investigate rewriting and optimization techniques for sticky-join sets of TGDs, and alternative forms of rewriting such as positive-existential queries. We also plan to develop improved techniques for rewriting an ontological query into a non-recursive Datalog program, rather than into a union of conjunctive queries (recall the discussion in Section ). While the current approaches yield exponentially large non-recursive Datalog programs, it is possible to rewrite queries and TBoxes into non-recursive Datalog programs whose size is simultaneously polynomial in the query and the TBox. This will be dealt in a forthcoming paper.	0.9
1112.0343	future_work	We also plan to develop improved techniques for rewriting an ontological query into a non-recursive Datalog program, rather than into a union of conjunctive queries (recall the discussion in Section ).	We plan to investigate rewriting and optimization techniques for sticky-join sets of TGDs, and alternative forms of rewriting such as positive-existential queries. We also plan to develop improved techniques for rewriting an ontological query into a non-recursive Datalog program, rather than into a union of conjunctive queries (recall the discussion in Section ). While the current approaches yield exponentially large non-recursive Datalog programs, it is possible to rewrite queries and TBoxes into non-recursive Datalog programs whose size is simultaneously polynomial in the query and the TBox. This will be dealt in a forthcoming paper.	0.9
1112.3187	open_problem	It is an open question asked in {{cite:2376872b5f9d42ac7008f364f4c4b2150bfa7799}} (on page 136) that given {{formula:596dd58d-e558-462e-8e9a-a8e79564e4ac}} , whether there exists a constant {{formula:97883a62-3332-4f37-a2dc-0e1a22778fdc}}  such that {{formula:bdf84654-767b-4111-80b3-1d36de78a1cf}}	It is an open question asked in {{cite:2376872b5f9d42ac7008f364f4c4b2150bfa7799}} (on page 136) that given {{formula:596dd58d-e558-462e-8e9a-a8e79564e4ac}} , whether there exists a constant {{formula:97883a62-3332-4f37-a2dc-0e1a22778fdc}}  such that {{formula:bdf84654-767b-4111-80b3-1d36de78a1cf}}	0.9
1112.3187	limitation	It is easy to see that the answer is negative for matrix-valued functions with irregular filtration.	It is easy to see that the answer is negative for matrix-valued functions with irregular filtration. In the following, we show that the answer is negative even for matrix-valued dyadic martingales. Recall that Remark REF  already shows that the answer is negative if one considers the column norm {{formula:828e3f52-3759-45c6-87f6-7828014defbf}}  alone on the right hand side.	0.8
1201.2501	future_work	Design an {{formula:f3a415bc-3a13-433c-b67b-a1a24ea67472}} -time algorithm for general signals.	Design an {{formula:f3a415bc-3a13-433c-b67b-a1a24ea67472}} -time algorithm for general signals. Alternatively, prove that no such algorithm exists, under “reasonable” assumptions.The {{formula:4264eebd-38ea-412b-912b-5672318b1c98}}  lower bound for the sample complexity shows that the running time of our algorithm, {{formula:e3613f54-5603-4afb-88cb-a9163e0efb79}} , is equal to the sample complexity of the problem times (roughly) {{formula:e7fd7443-0e4f-4318-b32f-b74d6013f910}} . One could speculate that this logarithmic discrepancy is due to the need for using FFT to process the samples. Although we do not have any evidence for the optimality of our general algorithm, the “sample complexity times {{formula:e0bdf8a4-cec0-48c7-9405-dd9879d4e2c9}} ” bound appears to be a natural barrier to further improvements.	0.9
1201.2501	future_work	Reduce the sample complexity of the algorithms.	Reduce the sample complexity of the algorithms. Currently, the number of samples used by each algorithm is only bounded by their running times.	0.8
1202.0667	open_problem	It is not known whether this is true for bipartite graphs.	It is not known whether this is true for bipartite graphs. It is not even known if {{formula:4d63c97e-dc8a-41c8-a2f9-8c29364d604d}}  is bounded for bipartite graphs. A heuristic argument is that the statement of the conjecture holds trivially if we extend the set of labels to real numbers. Indeed, any proper coloring of a {{formula:e1e9782b-aeb4-4b97-bf79-587fda668493}} -colorable graph {{formula:f84c9cf5-f174-470a-a68d-ed8243d85b14}}  with a set of {{formula:150bb41f-d220-4ca6-88f6-6d921541d65d}}  real numbers which is independent over rationals, gives an additive coloring of {{formula:4e233154-cc5b-48af-b031-b8ec10165020}} . Another direction is to consider additive colorings in finite Abelian groups.	0.9
1202.0667	open_problem	If true this is best possible, as we proved in section 5.	Conjecture 13 Every graph {{formula:b4fe5a52-35dd-4ff8-83ea-a0e05dba019d}}  has an additive coloring modulo {{formula:a8ffe386-a980-41c1-94fb-64defbdc134f}} .  If true this is best possible, as we proved in section 5.	0.7
1202.1054	limitation	it is almost definitely not enough to use only a morphological analyzer and a medium-sized unannotated corpus for this purpose; disregarding sentences with more than one verb leads to misleading view of the language as a whole, and selection preferences learned from these sentences would not be suitable for parsing most sentences.	While the problem of discovering grammatical subcategories of verbs remains interesting, and solutions to that problem would be of practical use, it is almost definitely not enough to use only a morphological analyzer and a medium-sized unannotated corpus for this purpose; disregarding sentences with more than one verb leads to misleading view of the language as a whole, and selection preferences learned from these sentences would not be suitable for parsing most sentences. To make better use of the existing data, it would be helpful to have a chunker to find the boundaries of clauses and noun phrases, as in {{cite:84562dd6e84c24baa16efff0bed45f94624901ed}}. For Czech verbs, Bojar similarly used finite-state rules to find coordinated and subordinate clauses bojar:2003.	0.9
1202.1054	future_work	This could be mitigated with software like MADA+TOKAN habash:2009, which chooses the most likely morphological analysis for a given context; for other languages, such as Quechua, similar tools will need to be developed.	While ambiguity in morphological analysis can be a hurdle in any language, the vowel-free nature of typical Arabic text presents a particularly serious one; we would like to be able to use any available unannotated text, but without morphological disambiguation, we are left with many possible interpretations for most tokens. This could be mitigated with software like MADA+TOKAN habash:2009, which chooses the most likely morphological analysis for a given context; for other languages, such as Quechua, similar tools will need to be developed.	0.85
1202.1331	future_work	Little is known about the behavior of the functions {{formula:bd7d0363-3804-4d2c-9e67-784130a2bbfb}} , {{formula:88af0c45-71e7-4a00-972d-cbf35517e768}} , and {{formula:5e2f5929-297c-4f0f-91a1-7b030eb73b2c}} .	– Little is known about the behavior of the functions {{formula:bd7d0363-3804-4d2c-9e67-784130a2bbfb}} , {{formula:88af0c45-71e7-4a00-972d-cbf35517e768}} , and {{formula:5e2f5929-297c-4f0f-91a1-7b030eb73b2c}} .	0.9
1202.1331	future_work	Providing more direct (i.e., less analytic) proofs for these results would likely be quite enlightening.	– Providing more direct (i.e., less analytic) proofs for these results would likely be quite enlightening.	0.8
1202.2591	future_work	The first is to make a connection to the relatively new field of homotopy type theory (HoTT) (see {{cite:0dac6ba5791631780ab426c0a70bba405bd70f8b}},{{cite:847e79ffe6d3ad0200d6ef07f208fa20819e96d4}}).	There are a few interesting directions for future research. The first is to make a connection to the relatively new field of homotopy type theory (HoTT) (see {{cite:0dac6ba5791631780ab426c0a70bba405bd70f8b}},{{cite:847e79ffe6d3ad0200d6ef07f208fa20819e96d4}}). The idea is that instead of two paths through a database schema being equal, one could declare them merely equivalent; if paths are declared equivalent in more than one way, these equivalences may also be declared as equivalent (or not). In this context, two observations on data may not be definitionally equal, but provably equal, and we consider the proofs and the differences between proofs as part of the data. To make this connection, the schema of a database should be a quasi-category ({{cite:b6333a447598473403d06d04c36143b6ff5d7473}},{{cite:c543cc327d7b0b1bd5521dd2f602c0779236f273}}) {{formula:f6b6c90d-67b1-4d8a-a55c-7f59d6ba177b}}  rather than an ordinary category. Each higher simplex encodes a proof that different paths (or paths of paths, etc.) through the schema are equivalent. We might replace the instance data by a functor (map of quasi-categories) {{formula:67ea307a-859a-4ca7-9b98-5fdafc975d13}} , where {{formula:81e78818-3230-47cd-a856-b5c02b61896a}}  is the quasi-category of homotopy types. In this context, classical homotopical questions, e.g. from the theory of model categories ({{cite:1e7f900e82ba0e503bafd7056398bbabab2fea01}}) may be even more applicable.	0.9
1202.2591	future_work	The question is whether these invariants would be meaningful and useful.	"Another direction for future research is to use topological tools to investigate or “mine"" data. For example, given a functor {{formula:813b19dc-fa01-49b8-bc40-00faea37816d}} , we can compose with the functor {{formula:b4ecda00-059d-4109-963f-00754dd5b0b7}}  which sends each set to the corresponding discrete topological space. The homotopy colimit of {{formula:841731a1-1eb7-472e-ac06-e614dbb1c369}}  is a topological space, of possibly any dimension and homotopy type, that encodes the connection pattern of the data. This space is homotopy equivalent to the nerve of the data bundle, {{formula:f7c223a5-a18b-4023-b9c9-30fd588491ce}} (see {{cite:61e20f53721ebe845857e406ba7afa9b18f9c5ce}}). Thus we could report homotopy invariants of the data {{formula:b4151dfb-6840-471a-935d-cf859db8f618}} , such as connected components, loops, etc. The question is whether these invariants would be meaningful and useful. For schemas of classical mathematical interest, such as the simplicial indexing category {{formula:728589bb-f811-48eb-bf42-18fc98928b76}} , the homotopy colimit of {{formula:6abf9302-07a5-4bdd-90fa-8744c804595b}}  is exactly what we want; it is the geometric realization of {{formula:2a767d33-e834-48a7-9a60-d9ef80beb1bd}} . It remains to be seen whether such homotopy invariants may be useful in other contexts; e.g. there may be some connection to the analysis given by persistent homology (see {{cite:46a3d501e9ec4fcdeb869413f855c7d16d2d4ddb}},{{cite:e77e8982fce545a025c37d7e19b14a5ba6c269df}})."	0.85
cs/0610128	limitation	Some implementation issues were not addressed.	Some implementation issues were not addressed. For example, many forms of buffering using finite-accuracy floating-point numbers are susceptible to significant numerical errors.	0.9
quant-ph/0402095	open_problem	Can quantum computers solve {{formula:2de743ab-f30f-4f20-85d1-cba9cc2299f0}} -complete problems in polynomial time?	Can quantum computers solve {{formula:2de743ab-f30f-4f20-85d1-cba9cc2299f0}} -complete problems in polynomial time?  In the early days of quantum computing, Bennett et al. {{cite:5a0a45badcd919053d8a7a3922bd95e2fdb29e2d}} gave an oracle relative to which {{formula:a65f7339-f8b9-49d2-b9ed-ff373112a988}} , providing what is still the best evidence we have that the answer is no.  It is easy to extend Bennett et al.'s result to give an oracle relative to which {{formula:7de710c4-f088-4bbe-94bc-3393b935d9d6}} ; that is, {{formula:0feaf9aa-32ae-404f-8389-b73f910adf64}}  is hard even for nonuniform quantum algorithms.  But when we try to show {{formula:9e42b679-736c-4860-8a4a-a21221c28022}}  relative to an oracle, a new difficulty arises: even if the oracle encodes {{formula:408e632b-a4bb-4177-9eb9-213f2f60a6c8}}  exponentially hard search problems for each input length {{formula:e96c9337-84d0-448b-9d47-366ce5b41a60}} , the quantum advice, being an “exponentially large object” itself, might somehow encode information about all {{formula:5d9acaa0-a933-44cf-8bd8-aab0b7b2cc3d}}  problems.  We need to argue that even if so, only a miniscule fraction of that information can be extracted by measuring the advice.	0.9
quant-ph/0402095	limitation	But what about the direct product theorem itself?  It seems like it should be trivial to prove—for surely there are no devious correlations by which success in finding one marked item leads to success in finding all the others!  So it is surprising that even a weak direct product theorem eluded proof for years.	But what about the direct product theorem itself?  It seems like it should be trivial to prove—for surely there are no devious correlations by which success in finding one marked item leads to success in finding all the others!  So it is surprising that even a weak direct product theorem eluded proof for years.  In 2001, Klauck {{cite:888d098e5e870361a39ad04d41f5d2d0cf41c5d0}} gave an attempted proof using the hybrid method of Bennett et al. {{cite:5a0a45badcd919053d8a7a3922bd95e2fdb29e2d}}.  His motivation was to show a limitation of space-bounded quantum sorting algorithms.  Unfortunately, Klauck's proof is fallacious.Specifically, the last sentence in the proof of Lemma 5 in {{cite:888d098e5e870361a39ad04d41f5d2d0cf41c5d0}} (“Clearly this probability is at least {{formula:ec55e323-51e0-41ba-a80f-92031d5f9f3c}} ”) is not justified by what precedes it.	0.8
